  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1111 PM CDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nEarly morning radar shows showers and thunderstorms located\nacross Colorado. The activity should continue to propagate\neastward through the overnight hours. This activity may reach\nacross NW KS/WC KS and N KS by the early morning hours. This\nactivity is not expected to be strong or severe. As such, have\nthe highest pops across the northern zones during the morning\nhours. The CAMs show the potential for additional showers and\nthunderstorms developing across Colorado Sunday evening. This\nactivity may reach far southwest Kansas during the late evening\nhours. Again, this activity is not expected to be strong or\nsevere by the time it reaches into western Kansas. Otherwise,\nhighs will continue to remain seasonably hot with values peaking\nin the 90s. Lows will be in a mix of 60s to 70s. The same\nweather patttern with diurnal showers and thunderstorms is\nexpected by the models for Monday. Again, far western Kansas\nstands to see the highest pops, as is this area is in closer\nproximity to the diurnally developing convection across the high\nplains.\n\nLong range models and their ensemble counterparts shows the mid\nlevel ridge sinking southward across the southern Rockies by\nTuesday and Wednesday. Diurnally induced storms may ride the \nedge of this ridge and may propagate once again into western \nKansas. Tuesday stands with the highest pops and large areal \ncoverage of storms during the evening hours. A similar weather \npattern is forecast by the models Wednesday and possibly \nThursday. Otherwise, highs will remain seasonably hot with \nvalues in the 90s and lows in the 60s and 70s.",
